From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Abe Massry <amassry@linode.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 17:34:27 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 4/7] util: main loop, coroutines, RCU and thread pool without
 threads

The WASI build has a single thread and no file descriptors to poll,
and its coroutines cannot suspend (util/coroutine-wasi.c).  Adapt the
pieces that rely on threads or on blocking:

- main-loop.c: glib-wasi's GMainContext never dispatches sources, so
  poll the main and iohandler AioContexts directly.  When every vCPU
  is idle, sleep until the next timer deadline (at most 10 ms) instead
  of spinning; a backend can set wasi_idle_wait to wake up early.
  There are no signals to set up.
- async.c: a one-shot bottom half scheduled from a coroutine runs at
  once, so its result is ready when the coroutine "yields"; waking the
  running coroutine is then a no-op.
- graph-lock.c: such a bottom half may take the graph write lock in
  coroutine context, e.g. bdrv_co_unref_child() on qcow2_do_open()'s
  error path.  Nothing else can hold the lock then, so take it without
  draining.  Opening a damaged qcow2 image now fails with the proper
  error instead of an assertion failure.
- rcu.c: the call_rcu thread never runs, so nothing queued with
  call_rcu() was ever freed (old TB hash tables, FlatViews, ...).
  rcu_run_callbacks_inline() runs the callbacks from the main loop.
- thread-pool.c: run work items synchronously; there are no workers.
